<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of New Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1949 CE · capital Rabaul; later Lae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The former German New Guinea, governed by Australia under a League of Nations mandate, where the 1930s brought gold rushes and first contact with the Highlands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>An Australian force captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-new-guinea">German New Guinea</a> in 1914 and held it under military rule. In 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> granted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> a mandate over the territory, covering northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bougainville-island">Bougainville</a>, and civil government began in 1921 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a> as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>German plantations were expropriated and sold to Australian ex-servicemen. Gold strikes at Edie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulolo">Bulolo</a> from 1926 led to dredges being flown in by aircraft, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> a pioneer of air freight. In 1933 the Leahy brothers and patrol officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-taylor-patrol-officer">Jim Taylor</a> entered the densely populated Highlands, unknown to outsiders. Volcanic eruptions in 1937 led to plans to move the capital to Lae.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a> in January 1942 and made it its major base in the South Pacific. Civil administration was suspended until 1945, and in 1949 the territory, now a UN trust territory, was joined with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-papua">Papua</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-papua-and-new-guinea">Territory of Papua and New Guinea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-new-guinea.jpg" alt="Territory of New Guinea" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Territory_of_New_Guinea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rabaul; later Lae</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous religions, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Tok Pisin, Papuan and Austronesian languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mandated Territory of New Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=territory-of-new-guinea" aria-label="Territory of New Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8901 2335 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of New Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9221 2591L9094 2680M9221 2591L8774 3709M9221 2591L8529 1145M9221 2591L9061 2787"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9094 2680h0M8774 3709h0M8529 1145h0M9061 2787h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9221 2591h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9221 2591h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rabaul. <a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=territory-of-new-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
